Why these fields?
RefreshToken
│
├── id
│
├── authUserId
│
├── userId
│
├── tokenHash              ← NEVER raw token
│
├── expiresAt              ← token lifetime
│
├── revoked                ← logout/revocation
│
├── revokedAt
│
├── replacedByTokenId      ← token rotation
│
├── deviceInfo             ← session tracking
│
├── ipAddress              ← security audit
│
├── createdAt
└── updatedAt
Real production refresh flow
LOGIN
  │
  ├── Access Token
  │      ↓
  │   short lifetime
  │
  └── Refresh Token
         ↓
      random secure value
         ↓
      SHA-256 hash
         ↓
      Save hash in DB

When the client refreshes:

Client
  │
  │ refresh token
  ▼
Auth Service
  │
  ├── hash incoming token
  │
  ├── find tokenHash
  │
  ├── check revoked
  │
  ├── check expiration
  │
  └── rotate token
        │
        ├── revoke old token
        │
        └── create new refresh token

So if an attacker steals a database dump, they don't get directly usable refresh tokens.

One important point: tokenHash should be generated in TokenService, not inside this entity.